Name :
================
Loop Guard

Why we did this
================
Root Guard protects against rogue switches becoming the Root Bridge.

However, another problem can occur in STP environments.

A link may become unidirectional.

Example:

Switch A can send traffic to Switch B

BUT

Switch B cannot send traffic back to Switch A

As a result, BPDUs stop arriving on one side of the link.

STP may incorrectly assume the path to the Root Bridge has failed and move a previously blocked port into the Forwarding state.

This can create:

- Layer 2 Loops
- Broadcast Storms
- MAC Table Instability
- Network Outages

To prevent this issue, Cisco introduced Loop Guard.

Loop Guard ensures that a non-designated port does not incorrectly transition to the forwarding state when expected BPDUs suddenly disappear.

What will happen
================
1. STP is operating normally.
2. Switch expects to receive BPDUs.
3. Due to a unidirectional link failure, BPDUs stop arriving.
4. Instead of moving to Forwarding State:
   - Loop Guard activates.
   - Port enters Loop-Inconsistent State.
5. Traffic is blocked.
6. Loop is prevented.
7. When BPDUs return:
   - Port automatically recovers.

Example

Normal Operation
----------------

Switch A -------- Switch B
      \          /
       \        /
        Switch C

One redundant link is blocked.

STP operates normally.


Link Failure
------------

Switch B stops receiving BPDUs.

Without Loop Guard:

Blocked Port
      ↓
Forwarding

Result:
--------
Loop Created


With Loop Guard:

Blocked Port
      ↓
Loop-Inconsistent

Result:
--------
Loop Prevented

Advantages and Disadvantages of this, hence what came next
==========================================================

Advantages
----------
1. Prevents Layer 2 loops caused by unidirectional failures.
2. Prevents unexpected forwarding state transitions.
3. Protects STP topology.
4. Automatically recovers when BPDUs return.
5. Improves network stability.
6. Reduces risk of broadcast storms.
7. Recommended for redundant Layer 2 environments.

Disadvantages
-------------
1. Requires proper STP design.
2. Only works on non-designated ports.
3. Does not detect physical link failures directly.
4. May temporarily block traffic during inconsistencies.
5. Additional STP features may still be required.

Hence what came next
--------------------
Loop Guard protects against missing BPDUs.

However, it does not actually detect whether a physical link has become unidirectional.

Administrators wanted a feature that directly detects one-way communication failures.

This led to:

UDLD (Unidirectional Link Detection)

which actively checks whether both sides of a link can communicate and disables the link if unidirectional communication is detected.

One-Shot Configuration
======================

Enable Loop Guard Globally

Switch(config)# spanning-tree loopguard default

OR


Enable Loop Guard Per Interface

Switch(config)# interface g0/1
Switch(config-if)# spanning-tree guard loop

Verification
============

show spanning-tree summary

show spanning-tree inconsistentports

show spanning-tree interface g0/1 detail

Expected Result
===============

Normal Operation:
-----------------
Port Operates Normally

BPDUs Missing:
--------------
Port Enters Loop-Inconsistent State

Layer 2 Loop:
-------------
Prevented

Broadcast Storm:
----------------
Prevented

Recovery:
---------
Automatic When BPDUs Return

Summary
=======

Problem:
A unidirectional link failure can cause STP to incorrectly move a blocked port into forwarding state, creating a loop.

Solution:
Loop Guard keeps the port in a protected Loop-Inconsistent state when expected BPDUs disappear.

Benefit:
Prevents Layer 2 loops and improves STP stability.

Limitation:
Does not directly detect one-way physical link failures.

Next Practical:
UDLD (Unidirectional Link Detection)
